## Spec review — issue #73

**(c) Implementation that looks wrong**

The SKILL.md rebase step (`.agents/skills/implement-epic/SKILL.md` lines 123-127) unconditionally does:
```
git fetch origin <trunk>
git rebase origin/<trunk>
```
regardless of whether `<N>` is a standalone issue or an epic sub-issue. But the doc's own step-3 preamble (line 111) explicitly splits fixed points by case: *"use `origin/epic/<epic-N>-<slug>` for an epic sub-issue, `origin/<trunk>` for a standalone issue"* — because a sub-issue's feature branch is cut from, and PR's back into, the **epic branch** (step 2: `base_branch=epic/<epic-N>-<slug>`; step 4: PR targets `epic/<epic-N>-<slug>`), not trunk. Line 158 confirms step 3 (which now includes this rebase) is meant to "apply identically" to both cases — yet rebasing an epic sub-issue's branch directly onto trunk detaches it from the epic branch's own commits (any earlier merged sub-issues), corrupts the fixed point the sandbox is about to read from `.sandcastle/base-branch.txt` (still set to the epic branch per line 111, but now diffed against a history that no longer contains it), and risks reintroducing conflicts/duplicate commits when the PR later merges into the epic branch. The spec's AC — *"rebases the feature branch onto trunk before invoking the sandbox"* — is followed to the letter, but the letter conflicts with this repo's own two-tier epic model that the rest of the same document maintains. This is exactly the kind of silent-corruption failure mode the epic (#64) is trying to eliminate, just relocated to the epic-branch case.

**(a) Missing/partial**

Everything else in the AC list is present: host-side `assertGuardrailsPresent()` runs before `ensureContainerCacheDirs()`/`startHostBrowser()`/`run()` in both `runSandcastle()` and `runSandcastleForIssue()` (`scripts/run-sandcastle.ts` lines 560-563, 613-622), the thrown message names the missing artifact(s), and both present/absent cases are unit-tested (`scripts/run-sandcastle.test.ts`). One minor partial: AC says *"verified by the fact that no container is started on the failure path"* — there's no test actually asserting this ordering end-to-end (e.g. that `runSandcastle` never reaches `ensureContainerCacheDirs`/`run()` when guardrails are missing); it's only true by inspection of call order, not locked in by a test.

**(b) Scope creep**

None found — `assertGuardrailsPresent()` is called in two places, but that mirrors the pre-existing `assertOnIssueBranch()` duplication pattern and is explained in an inline comment, not unrequested behavior.
