feat(#73): fail loudly when a branch is missing the guardrails

Sandcastle bind-mounts the feature branch's worktree, so guardrail
delivery has always depended on branch ancestry rather than wall-clock: a
branch cut before a guardrail file landed upstream bind-mounts a tree that
never saw it, runs unguarded with no signal, and can even delete the
guardrail on merge if the diffstat goes unnoticed.

Two halves:

- scripts/run-sandcastle.ts: assertGuardrailsPresent() checks a fixed list
  of guardrail artifacts (.sandcastle/efficiency-rules.md, AGENTS.md,
  CLAUDE.md — the ones still delivered by bind-mount rather than baked
  into the image per #67) exist in the checked-out tree, and throws
  naming whatever's missing. Wired into both runSandcastle() and
  runSandcastleForIssue(), before any container/browser/gh work, mirroring
  assertOnIssueBranch()'s existing dual-check pattern.

- .agents/skills/implement-epic/SKILL.md: documents rebasing the feature
  branch onto its base_branch (trunk for a standalone issue, the epic
  branch for a sub-issue — never trunk directly for a sub-issue, which
  would detach it from sibling sub-issues already merged into the epic
  branch) before invoking the sandbox, so a branch cut before a guardrail
  edit landed upstream picks it up. A rebase conflict aborts and hands off
  via the hand-back procedure rather than resolving unattended.

Reviewed against origin/epic/64-sandcastle-guardrails via two independent
sub-agents (Standards, Spec). Standards came back clean. Spec caught a
real defect in an earlier draft — rebasing sub-issue branches onto trunk
directly rather than the epic branch — fixed by rebasing onto base_branch
instead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
EOF
)")
